{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cg8q4GE5oR7ABSnKnAXi2","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I've finished ticket 03 and checked all five items. The full `npm test` passes (4373 tests), lint is clean, and everything is committed in `365e85e` and `25efd95`.\n\n**Engine changes** (`src/engine/index.js`):\n- **Holds counted on arrival:** `wt-start-server` creates the torrent's server entry and counts its hold straight away. The server itself is only created once the torrent is ready. Only the first early request waits for `ready`; later ones just add a holder.\n- **Release in any state:** `wt-stop-server` takes away one holder whether the torrent is ready or not. At zero the entry is deleted. If the torrent becomes ready after that, or the server finishes starting after that, nothing happens.\n- **One way to drop an entry:** the last release, shutdown and the torrent's close handler from ticket 02 all go through `forgetServer`. In the close handler it runs after the duplicate-torrent guard, so a duplicate add being destroyed leaves the live torrent's server alone.\n- **No dead servers on the torrent:** destroying a server also removes it from webtorrent's own server list for that torrent.\n- **Unknown torrent unchanged:** `wt-start-server` for a torrent the engine doesn't have still counts no hold and sends no reply.\n\n**Tests:**\n- **Real sidecar** (`test/unit/engine-entry.js`): four new tests that check whether the reported port accepts a connection. The burst test and the resume test both failed on the old code, for the reasons the ticket describes. The two counting tests pass on old and new code, so they guard against regressions rather than proving the fix. The new spawn-and-connect helper can be reused for other sidecar cases.\n- **Source scan** (`test/unit/engine-torrent-close-wiring.js`): pins that the close handler forgets the server entry after the duplicate guard.\n\n**How the burst test differs from the ticket's wording:**\n- **Not quite one burst:** the test starts the torrent from a `.torrent` path, which the engine reads asynchronously. A `wt-start-server` sent in the same burst would find no torrent and pass without testing anything. So the test waits for `wt-parsed`, then sends start and stop together, and asserts that `ready` hadn't arrived yet.\n- **More for the fixture to verify:** missing files verify in milliseconds, so `ready` arrived first even then. As the ticket suggested, the test writes a full-length empty (sparse) copy of the file, which has to be read and hashed before `ready`. It passed 5 runs in a row.\n- **Counting tests run after `ready`:** they need the URL from the server's reply. Counting before `ready` is covered by the burst test.\n\nRemoving the server from webtorrent's private server list is not tested, as the spec says. I added the server lifecycle and the sidecar test pitfalls to `src/engine/AGENTS.md`, and the ticket notes are in `.nightralph/notes.md` (uncommitted)."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":231,"cache_read_input_tokens":95759,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":231},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"51376cad-72e5-4753-a12b-f4c0880ced8a","uuid":"39e16ecd-99b3-46e6-80f4-6b3740f4e714","timestamp":"2026-10-18T01:24:24.766Z","request_id":"req_011Cg8q4FqGGzPYCBoDaMhDq"}
